WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.242
The address side has the same
problem as the name side.

2
00:00:03.242 --> 00:00:06.727
Every cloud and NSX allocates
addresses on its own,

3
00:00:06.727 --> 00:00:12.295
so a range created in a console can overlap
on-premises space or another division,

4
00:00:12.295 --> 00:00:15.204
and nobody knows until a conflict appears.

5
00:00:15.204 --> 00:00:17.093
Integration depth varies.

6
00:00:17.093 --> 00:00:21.795
AWS can hand authority for private
address scopes to an external IPAM,

7
00:00:21.795 --> 00:00:25.303
with Infoblox as the book's
example, but not in GovCloud,

8
00:00:25.303 --> 00:00:27.953
so it doesn't help a
GovCloud estate today.

9
00:00:27.953 --> 00:00:32.216
Azure and Google Cloud can't hand
off, so you mirror and reconcile.

10
00:00:32.216 --> 00:00:37.676
The rule: reserve blocks in the enterprise
IPAM before any cloud or NSX network is

11
00:00:37.676 --> 00:00:39.809
created, integrate where you can,

12
00:00:39.809 --> 00:00:43.047
reconcile where you can't,
and monitor sync health.

13
00:00:43.047 --> 00:00:47.040
And plan IPv4 and IPv6 together.
